```c
int fibonacci(int n)
{
  // This implementation uses the fast doubling algorithm,
  // which computes the nth Fibonacci number in O(log n) iterations.
  // Since the loop bound is fixed (32 iterations for a 32‐bit integer),
  // we can fully unroll the loop to target minimal latency.

  int a = 0;
  int b = 1;
  int msb = 31;

  // Find the index of the most significant set bit in n.
  // (For n==0, msb becomes -1 and the loop below will not update a, so return a=0.)
  while(msb >= 0 && (((n >> msb) & 1) == 0))
  {
    msb--;
  }

  // Iterate over all 32 bits, performing computations only when i <= msb.
  // Full unrolling ensures that the entire loop is implemented as combinational logic,
  // minimizing latency.
  for (int i = 31; i >= 0; i--)
  {
    #pragma hls_unroll yes
    if(i <= msb)
    {
      int c = a * (2 * b - a);  // c = F(2k)
      int d = a * a + b * b;    // d = F(2k+1)
      if ((n >> i) & 1)
      {
        a = d;
        b = c + d;
      }
      else
      {
        a = c;
        b = d;
      }
    }
  }

  return a;
}
```

This version preserves the functionality (computing the nth Fibonacci number, with F(0)=0 and F(1)=1) while reducing the latency by replacing an O(n) loop with a fully-unrolled O(log n) loop.
